\description{
The biotype split used by the workflow (protein coding versus retained
intron, nonsense-mediated decay, ...) is a proxy. These functions make
productivity a computed, graded property of every transcript and
summarise it per gene:
\itemize{
\item \code{transcript_productivity()} classifies each assayed transcript as
\code{"productive"} (complete ORF, no predicted NMD, same ORF as the
canonical isoform), \code{"altered_productive"} (complete ORF, no NMD, but
a different ORF than the canonical isoform: alternative start, in-frame
change, frameshift or stop in novel sequence), \code{"unproductive"} (no
ORF, premature stop codon by the 50-nt rule, no stop codon, an
unproductive biotype such as \code{nonsense_mediated_decay},
\code{retained_intron}, \code{protein_coding_CDS_not_defined},
\code{protein_coding_LoF}, or a longest ORF without coding potential),
\code{"noncoding"} (transcripts of genes without any coding isoform) or
\code{"uncertain"} (incomplete CDS annotation without
a premature stop, a credible ORF unrelated to the canonical one, FANTOM
\code{uncertain_coding}).
The \code{reason} column explains each call and \code{source} tells whether it
came from the sequence (\code{"sequence"}, when the annotation has
transcript sequences, see \code{\link[=extract_transcript_sequences]{extract_transcript_sequences()}} and
\code{\link[=transcript_orfs]{transcript_orfs()}}), the annotation (\code{"annotation"}) or external
evidence (\code{"evidence"}). Evidence columns take precedence, in this
order: a \code{productivity_evidence} column added with
\code{\link[=add_annotation_columns]{add_annotation_columns()}}, a validated NMD exon (\code{\link[=add_nmd_exons]{add_nmd_exons()}}),
a Ribo-seq ORF matching the transcript's ORF (\code{\link[=add_riboseq_orfs]{add_riboseq_orfs()}}),
an APPRIS principal isoform (\code{\link[=add_appris]{add_appris()}}).
\item \code{productive_fraction()} is the share of a gene's expression carried by
productive (and altered productive) isoforms, per sample or per group,
with the difference between the last and the first group level. A
gene whose expression is flat while its productive fraction drops is a
post-transcriptional regulation call that gene-level differential
expression misses. With inferential replicates (\code{inf_reps}, Salmon
bootstraps or Gibbs samples) it reports the uncertainty of each
fraction, and with a \code{reference} (\code{\link[=gtex_reference]{gtex_reference()}}) the difference to
the healthy-tissue baseline.
\item \code{productivity_fractions()} splits the gene's expression over all
productivity classes and, for unproductive isoforms, over the
mechanism (\code{nmd}, \code{retained_intron}, \code{no_orf}, \code{non_stop}, \code{other}).
\item \code{test_productive_fraction()} tests the change in productive fraction
between two groups on counts, with a quasi-binomial model per gene
(pooled proportions per group, Pearson over-dispersion, t test on the
log odds ratio, BH adjustment). A gene with fewer than 5 reads in its
smaller class (productive or not), pooled over both groups, carries
too little information on usage: it gets a \code{log_odds_ratio} of 0 and no
statistic or p-value, and does not count in the BH adjustment. A gene
without reads in one group gets \code{NA} there too.
\item \code{run_productivity_enrichment()} ranks genes by the change in
productive fraction between the last and the first group level
(\code{rank_by = "delta"}), by that change over its standard deviation
(\code{rank_by = "delta_z"}) or by the statistic of
\code{test_productive_fraction()} (\code{rank_by = "statistic"}), and runs gene
set enrichment (\code{fgsea}) on gene symbols, which captures regulated
unproductive splicing (AS-NMD) directly. It takes an
\link{IsoformicExperiment}, a \code{productive_fraction()} table or a
\code{test_productive_fraction()} table, and one or several gene set
collections.
\item \code{poison_exons()} lists exons that occur only in unproductive isoforms
of genes that also have productive isoforms and that do not overlap
any exon of a productive isoform (cassette poison / NMD exons, as
opposed to retained introns or alternative splice sites);
\code{\link[=plot_genomic_context]{plot_genomic_context()}} outlines them with \code{highlight_poison_exons}.
\item \code{benchmark_productivity()} measures the rules against the annotation
biotypes: recall of \code{nonsense_mediated_decay} and \code{retained_intron}
transcripts by the sequence/structure rules alone, and the share of
\code{protein_coding} and canonical transcripts called productive.
\item \code{productivity_palette()} gives colours for the classes.
}
}
\details{
\strong{Low-count genes.} A productive fraction computed from a few reads is
mostly sampling noise: a gene with 4 reads moves by 0.25 when one read
changes isoform. \code{delta_z} does not catch this, because the inferential
replicates measure how the reads are split between isoforms, not how many
reads were sampled. \code{min_gene_count} removes genes whose mean count (the
sum of the counts of their isoforms in the \code{"counts"} assay) is below it in
any group, before the statistics and the BH adjustment; with
\code{rank_by = "statistic"}, before the ranking. The filter is off by default
(0); 10 reads is a reasonable start. The rule asks for enough reads in
every group, because a fraction is compared between groups: it is
stricter than the transcript filter of \code{\link[=test_usage]{test_usage()}} (\code{min_count} reads
in as many samples as the smallest group), and \code{min_expr} sets single
isoform values to zero rather than removing genes.
\code{rank_by = "statistic"} accounts for the reads of every gene and for the
variation between its samples. Its dispersion is estimated for each gene
alone, on \code{n - 2} degrees of freedom and not moderated, so with three
samples per group the ranking is still noisy. The t statistic is put on
the normal scale through its p-value
(\code{sign(statistic) * qnorm(pvalue / 2, lower.tail = FALSE)}), because the
degrees of freedom differ between genes when samples without reads drop
out. Its sign is that of the pooled change (\code{delta_productive_fraction}
of \code{test_productive_fraction()}), which can differ from the mean of the
per-sample fractions in \code{productive_fraction()} when the samples differ
much in depth. Genes with fewer than 5 reads in one class get no
statistic and are left out of the ranking, so that a change in the
expression of the whole gene does not rank it.

\strong{Uncertainty of the fractions.} With inferential replicates,
\code{productive_fraction()} computes the productive fraction of every gene and
sample in each draw. The draws are counts; for another \code{assay} each draw
is first put on the scale of that assay, multiplied by \code{assay / counts} of
the same transcript and sample (for TPM, the inverse of the effective
length times the scaling of the sample), so that the fractions of the
draws and of the assay, and \code{min_expr}, are on one scale. Where the point
count or value is zero (Gibbs draws often give reads to such cells) the
factor is estimated: from the \code{length} assay when the assay follows it
(TPM of \code{\link[=read_salmon]{read_salmon()}} and \code{\link[=read_oarfish]{read_oarfish()}}), otherwise from a transcript
effect plus a sample effect on the log scale fitted on the other cells,
and for a transcript without any count from its annotated length.
\code{productive_fraction_sd} is the sample standard deviation of a gene's
fraction over the draws, around the mean of the draws, averaged over the
samples kept; \code{delta_productive_fraction_sd} is the standard deviation
over the draws of the change between the group means, and \code{delta_z} is
\code{delta_productive_fraction} divided by it. In every draw, only the samples
in which the assay gives the gene a fraction are used. Both measure the
spread of the draws, not their distance to the point estimate: the mean of
Gibbs draws can differ from the EM estimate of the assay.
}
